messages:
Alex W: Heads-up — running into an annoying issue when using redwood-sdk-js in an edge function inside our pnpm monorepo. Vercel Edge throws a resolution/exports error at runtime: `The requested module ... does not provide an export named 'default'`. Works fine when I bundle the function locally. Anyone hit weird ESM/CJS + pnpm workspace hoisting + edge runtime interaction? :thinking_face:

Maria G: Can you paste the sdk package.json (the one in packages/redwood-sdk-js)? especially "main", "module", "exports", and "types".

Alex W: Sure — trimmed. This is from packages/redwood-sdk-js/package.json:

```json
{
  "name": "redwood-sdk-js",
  "version": "0.12.0-next",
  "main": "dist/cjs/index.js",
  "module": "dist/esm/index.mjs",
  "types": "dist/types/index.d.ts",
  "exports": {
    ".": {
      "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js",
      "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs"
    }
  }
}
```

Chris L: That exports map is the right idea. Edge runtimes (and some bundlers) expect an ESM entrypoint. Vercel Edge will try to import the package as ESM and can trip over workspace symlinks from pnpm. Two things to check: 1) are you importing the package as `import SDK from 'redwood-sdk-js'` or doing a path import? 2) what's the layout in the deployed lambda (is `dist/esm/index.mjs` actually there)?

Alex W: We do `import { Client } from 'redwood-sdk-js'` in the edge file. dist has both esm and cjs built. Deploy artifact (zip) includes modules under `.pnpm/.../node_modules/redwood-sdk-js/dist/esm/index.mjs`. Error suggests the runtime can't find exported names.

Karthik R: pnpm creates a nested node_modules structure and uses symlinks — edge platform will load the package via the runtime loader which sometimes sees the symlink root, and Node's ESM exports resolution is strict about the package boundary. A few pragmatic fixes we've used: 
- publish an explicit ESM-only entrypoint for edge (`exports.worker` or `exports.edge`) and import that path in edge code. e.g. `import { Client } from 'redwood-sdk-js/edge'`.
- or ship a root ESM file that re-exports the real esm entry so the engine sees a top-level ESM module `dist/esm/index.mjs` not sitting behind a symlink.

Sam T: Example exports map we've used for dual cjs/esm + edge: 

```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "types": "./dist/types/index.d.ts",
    "import": "./dist/esm/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/cjs/index.js"
  },
  "./edge": {
    "import": "./dist/edge/index.mjs"
  }
}
```

Also add a top-level "types": "dist/types/index.d.ts" field so TS consumers pick it up.

Maria G: Another pnpm-specific workaround — in consumer apps where you run edge code, set `shamefully-hoist=true` in `.npmrc` during build to flatten deps, or use `public-hoist-pattern` to hoist redwood-sdk-js. Ugly but works as a short-term. Longer term: publish an explicit edge entrypoint so we don't rely on hoisting.

Alex W: Hmm. We don't want to force customers to hoist. I can add the `./edge` entry and export a small ESM wrapper that only imports the minimal runtime code (no CJS). Would `import { Client } from 'redwood-sdk-js/edge'` be accepted by bundlers and Vercel?

Chris L: Yes, bundlers will resolve that if you include the `./edge` export. For Vercel Edge specifically, prefer `.mjs` ESM file targeted at esm. Also ensure package.json has `type: "module"` for the edge entry or use `.mjs` extension. Example edge wrapper (dist/edge/index.mjs):

```js
// tiny wrapper that re-exports only ESM-friendly symbols
export { Client } from '../esm/index.mjs';
```

Karthik R: FYI for TypeScript consumers in workspaces: TS's module resolution + package exports sometimes hides types. Add `typesVersions` in package.json to map TS imports to the right `.d.ts` if you support both path imports and subpath (`/edge`). Example: 

```json
"typesVersions": {
  "*": {
    "*": ["dist/types/*"]
  }
}
```

Alex W: I tried adding `./edge` export and shipped a patch build. Deploy still failed with the same message but stack trace shows the edge runtime tried to load the package from the symlinked path under `.pnpm` and resolved the CJS file first.

Sam T: Double-check the consumer's build/bundler config. If you use esbuild for Vercel, ensure external packages are not forced to require() the CJS build. In our nextjs builder we added an explicit alias for edge import: 

```js
// next.config.js (example)
module.exports = {
  webpack: (config) => {
    config.resolve.alias['redwood-sdk-js/edge'] = path.resolve(__dirname, 'node_modules/redwood-sdk-js/dist/edge/index.mjs');
    return config;
  }
}
```

Maria G: Another quick test: create a minimal repro app outside the monorepo (plain npm install redwood-sdk-js) and deploy the same edge file to Vercel. If it works there, it's a workspace/pnpm issue.

Alex W: Good idea — I'll spin up a tiny repro.

pkg-bot: A release build (0.12.0-next.1) with an explicit ./edge export was pushed to the internal registry at 1712001300.

Alex W: Repro outside the monorepo worked. That confirms pnpm/workspace layout is the culprit. Going to try `shamefully-hoist` in CI build for now and open a proper fix to publish a dedicated edge entry that doesn't rely on symlinks. Thanks for the pointers — saved me a day. :relieved:

Chris L: Sounds good. When you open the PR for the dedicated edge entry, include a small test that imports `redwood-sdk-js/edge` in a Vercel Edge function and runs in the edge runtime CI job.

Karthik R: And add a short note to the monorepo SDK readme about pnpm pitfalls and a recommended package.json `exports` layout.

Alex W: Will do. Follow-up PR and CI link incoming.

Sam T: If anyone else hits odd TS types not resolving for `/edge`, ping me — we can add typesVersions and a tiny declaration file re-exporting the right types.

Alex W: Thanks all.
